<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otho de la Roche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1234</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Othon de la Roche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otho de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> was a Burgundian knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Thebes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> in 1205 and became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lord of Athens. He also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. Around 1225 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, leaving his Greek lands to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otho-de-la-roche.jpg" alt="Otho de la Roche" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original design: unknown 13th-century artist; Sketch: Gustave Schlumberger (1844–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Othon_de_la_Roche_(Schlumberger,_1897).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The chronicler Alberic of Trois-Fontaines calls Otho the son of a noble Pons of La Roche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Since Burgundy may mean either the Free County or the Duchy, the place is uncertain, but La Roche-sur-l'Ognon, a hamlet by Rigney in the Free County near his family's other lands, is the likeliest. He confirmed his forefathers' gifts to Charlieu Abbey in 1195. Geoffrey of Villehardouin places him in the sixth division of the crusading army, formed by the people of Burgundy, when it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1203, and later names him as one of the four chief counsellors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> in the quarrels over the division of the conquered empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Athens Otho took the title Megaskyr, or Grand Lord. Whether he was ever officially duke is uncertain: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> says the title was first granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> to his successor around 1259, though a letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> of July 1208 already calls Otho duke. The Acropolis was fortified in his time, and he settled Cistercian monks from Bellevaux Abbey at Daphni Monastery in about 1207. After Boniface died in September 1207 he stayed loyal to the Latin Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a>, attending the parliaments of Ravennika in 1209 and 1210, though the historian Jean Longnon holds that he never accepted Henry as his direct overlord.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry, became the economic centre of his lands, while he lived in his castle on the Acropolis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> clergy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> complained of him often, and he was accused of treating Greek priests as serfs. In 1214 he gave the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livadeia">Livadeia</a> to the pope and took it back as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> for a yearly tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a> excommunicated him and laid his lands under interdict, and in about 1223 he agreed to return church lands while keeping their furnishings for an annual payment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otho is not mentioned after 1225. He was dead by 1234, when his son Othon II of Ray called himself son of the late lord Othon, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. His wife, Isabel, is generally described as heiress of the lordship of Ray-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, though Longnon made her a daughter of Clarembaud IV of Chappes. Some of the family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, among them a great-grandson, Walter, who was a member of the chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> in 1292.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Othon de la Roche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1205 Boniface of Montferrat granted Athens and Thebes to the Burgundian knight <b>Otho de la Roche</b>, who took the title Lord of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a></li><li><blockquote>The Principality of Achaea granted them to <b>Otho de la Roche</b>, lord of the Duchy of Athens, who held them as a fief of the prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-argos-and-nauplia">Lordship of Argos and Nauplia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doubs">Doubs</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Haute-Saône, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.17,6.42&amp;year=1224" aria-label="Doubs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Otho de la Roche"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5372 6070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5372 6070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.17,6.42&amp;year=1224">What was here in 1224? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
